data: {"type":"done","contentUnchanged":false,"content":"Three days is the sweet spot for the South Rim — enough time to hit the big overlooks, get below the rim on foot, and still catch two sunrises/sunsets without feeling rushed.\n\n## At a glance\n- Base yourself in Grand Canyon Village on the South Rim — it's the hub for all three days, and the free shuttle system covers most of what's below.\n- Standard entrance fee runs $30 per private vehicle, good for 7 days.\n- No permit is required for anything in this plan — day hikes on Bright Angel and South Kaibab don't need one. Check permits on TrailVerse if you're considering an overnight below the rim, which does require a backcountry permit.\n- There's no timed-entry requirement at Grand Canyon, which means no crowd control — parking lots at Mather Point and the Village fill by mid-morning most of the year. Get to the rim before 7 AM for sunrise spots and easy parking.\n- August days typically run hot on the rim (mid-80s to low 90s°F) and can be 20+ degrees hotter if you drop below the rim — carry more water than you think you need, and start below-rim hikes at first light.\n- Worth a quick look at current alerts for Grand Canyon on TrailVerse before you go — road and shuttle conditions shift with construction season.\n\n Day 1 — South Rim Highlights & Orientation\nEase in with the classic rim views and one below-the-rim taste of the canyon before the afternoon heat builds.\n\n Grand Canyon Visitor Center (Mather Point area)\nStart here around 8 AM — grab a map, check ranger program times, and get the lay of the land before the lots fill up.\n\n Mather Point\nA 2-minute walk from the visitor center and the first big \"whoa\" moment. Don't linger too long — it gets packed by mid-morning.\n\n Rim Trail (Mather Point to Yavapai Point segment)\n1.5 miles one-way, mostly paved, flat — Easy, ~40 minutes. Quieter than the overlooks and gives you a rolling view the whole way.\n\n Bright Angel Lodge (dining area)\nLunch stop. Grab something quick at the fountain or a sit-down meal before your afternoon hike.\n\n Bright Angel Trail (to 1.5-Mile Resthouse)\n3 miles round trip, ~1,100 ft gain, ~2.5 hours — Moderate, dirt/gravel switchbacks with some exposure. Water available at the resthouse. Turn around here — this is a solid below-rim taste without overextending on day one.\n\n Hopi Point (sunset)\nTake the free shuttle out (no driving needed) and arrive 45 minutes before sunset — this is one of the best unobstructed western views on the rim, and it gets crowded fast once word gets out on clear evenings.\n\n Day 2 — Desert View Drive & Below-Rim Views\nA longer day mixing an early below-rim hike with the scenic drive east toward Desert View.\n\n South Kaibab Trail (to Ooh Aah Point)\n1.8 miles round trip, ~760 ft gain, ~1.5 hours — Moderate, steep and exposed with no shade or water on trail. Start by 6:30 AM to beat the heat — this trailhead has no parking lot, so take the shuttle.\n\n Grand Canyon Village dining (Market Plaza area)\nRefuel and pick up extra water/snacks before heading out on Desert View Drive.\n\n Grandview Point\nAbout 12 miles east of the Village, roughly 20 minutes by car. One of the most dramatic and least crowded overlooks along the drive.\n\n Moran Point\nA quick stop a few miles further east — good light for photos, especially midday.\n\n Desert View Watchtower\nThe eastern anchor of the drive, about 25 miles / 45 minutes from the Village. Climb the tower for one of the best panoramic views in the park.\n\n Desert View Trading Post & Snack Bar\nGrab a late lunch or snack here before heading back — last services before you loop back toward the Village.\n\n Rim Trail (Village to Powell Point sunset segment)\nEasy, mostly flat walk back near the Village. Time it to land at an overlook 30-40 minutes before sunset.\n\n Day 3 — Sunrise, Museum & Departure\nA lighter final day built around sunrise and packing up.\n\n Yaki Point (sunrise)\nShuttle-only access (no private vehicles). Arrive 30 minutes before sunrise — quieter than Mather Point and one of the best eastern-facing views on the South Rim.\n\n El Tovar Dining Room\nA proper sit-down breakfast at the historic lodge to close out the trip right.\n\n Yavapai Geology Museum\nShort, easy stop — small museum with big windows framing the canyon and good exhibits on how it formed. 30-45 minutes is plenty.\n\n Hermit Road overlooks (Powell Point, Hopi Point revisit)\nRide the shuttle out for a last look before you head out — a good low-key way to spend your last few hours before departure.\n\n## Don't Forget\n- **Water**: at least 1 liter per hour of hiking, more below the rim in August heat\n- **Sun protection**: hat, sunscreen, sunglasses — there's minimal shade on any of these trails\n- **Layers**: mornings can be cool even in summer; it warms up fast once the sun's up\n- **Shuttle map**: download the park shuttle route ahead of time — South Kaibab and Yaki Point have no public parking\n- **Cash/card for the Trading Post and snack bars**: some remote spots have limited connectivity for card readers\n- Cell service is spotty to nonexistent below the rim — let someone know your hiking plan before you drop in","responseId":"ae23f20c-aaf3-427b-9b13-793bf50a9c0c","responseVersion":1,"contentHash":"44414b390a95690a76cb50b370499cbd8304c695155e1c78466810ac667e5e4b","itineraryHash":"4ca070ba77fed023cf60c18d1f422873f40cb091f7df0eb51c2b6953635a6789","canonicalPlan":{"type":"itinerary","version":1,"generatedAt":"2026-08-06T16:47:34.179Z","createdFrom":"ai","parkName":"Grand Canyon National Park","parkCode":"grca","responseId":"ae23f20c-aaf3-427b-9b13-793bf50a9c0c","responseVersion":1,"contentHash":"44414b390a95690a76cb50b370499cbd8304c695155e1c78466810ac667e5e4b","itineraryHash":"4ca070ba77fed023cf60c18d1f422873f40cb091f7df0eb51c2b6953635a6789","days":[{"id":"day-1","dayNumber":1,"label":"Day 1 — South Rim Highlights & Orientation","stops":[{"id":"stop-1-1","order":0,"type":"visitor_cent